    "result_text": "The build is green. Let me summarize what I implemented.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the business-day rule for the effective registration date in `OwnerRestControllerV1.java`.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`resolveRegistrationDate` now rolls weekends forward** (`OwnerRestControllerV1.java:318`). Previously it returned the supplied date or `LocalDate.now()` as-is. Now, in both cases, it passes the date through a new `toBusinessDay` helper that advances a Saturday or Sunday to the following Monday. Because this is the single point of resolution the controller already used, every derived value automatically picks up the adjusted date:\n   - the stored `registrationDate`,\n   - the membership number's year segment (`generateMembershipNumber` already receives this resolved date at line 146).\n\n2. **The daily create-limit now counts per adjusted business day** (`OwnerRestControllerV1.java:133`). Changed `countOwnersRegisteredOn(LocalDate.now())` to `countOwnersRegisteredOn(registrationDate)`, so the cap counts owners against the adjusted effective date rather than the raw server date.\n\n3. Added the `import java.time.DayOfWeek;` needed by the new helper.\n\n**Behavior change confirmed:** a supplied weekend date is now rolled forward too (not just the defaulted server date), matching the spec.\n\nThe test suite passes: `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 `BUILD SUCCESS`. The existing `coreDefaultDateIsBusinessDay` acceptance test is green.\n\nNote: I scoped the roll to weekends only (Sat/Sun \u2192 Monday), exactly as the spec states. The test harness's `toBusinessDay` helper also skips a pinned holiday set, but the specification for this change only concerns weekends, so I did not add holiday handling.",
